    <fr:title text="L (Language)">L (Language)</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    I vaguely recall reading that the tradition of isolating the non-logical
    symbols (constants, functions, relations) of a first order logic for a
    language <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {L}]]></fr:tex>, and referring to suitable models as
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {L}]]></fr:tex>-structures, was popularized by Carnap, and maybe in
    particular by <html:em>The Logical Syntax of Language</html:em>.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    I am unable to track down a source for this now. I'll have to check <html:em>The
    Syntax</html:em> sometime. I can say at least <html:em>Meaning and Necessity</html:em> does
    <html:em>not</html:em> make use of this terminology in the same way.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
